I occasionally do play the game completely drunk myself, but then, I
don't expect to win then!
But really, you make good points and I agree with you.  A melee
character should fight melee!

Of course dagger throwing is a great tactic--as stated above, it allows
you to hit without being hit.
But it is only a great tactic--not necessarily a great _strategy_.
I've had characters who tried the dagger route from
the get go only to find that the stack of daggers was weighing them
down.  I would estimate that
for a dagger-throwing approach to be fully successful, a stack of at
least about 10 daggers is
needed, since the risky situations usually involve multiple enemies.
But for a low-strength character (one
who's just starting off especially), this can cause inventory problems,
and isn't remaining unburdened a
good strategy?
And which characters are less likely to get burdened by dagger stacks?
Fighter characters,
of course!  Doesn't this show a game balance wherein the classes that
lack melee skills cannot simply
rely on massive dagger stacks to get by?
I see the counter argument that those classes that are able to carry
more daggers are meant to
do so, but the same classes are clearly also meant to melee.  Are
daggers generally more "optimal"?  Yes.
But often only as a reduction measure.  Masses of enemies are not best
(or even possibly) fought with daggers
alone, and let's not forget the situation of throwing daggers at, say,
an elf who might throw them right
back at you!

Anyway, both tactics have their merits, and for fighting classes, a
combined arms approach is best.
